    "culturalContext": "Phone numbers are a relatively modern addition to Korean dream symbolism — but they slot naturally into a very old tradition. Korean folk belief has long treated numbers as cosmic signals tied to fate rather than mere quantities. From ancient times, dreams in which an ancestor or divine spirit revealed a specific number were considered the most potent form of prophetic dreaming (예지몽, yeji-mong), a tradition still alive today in popular stories about lottery winners who chose numbers seen in dreams.\n\nThe cultural baggage individual digits carry within a phone number is central to the interpretation. The digit 4 is so persistently associated with death (死) that most Korean buildings still skip the fourth floor, labeling it 'F' instead. The digit 8, on the other hand, is considered the supreme lucky number representing prosperity and infinity — and it is common practice in Korean and Chinese business culture to seek phone numbers rich in 8s. The digit 9 carries auspiciousness as a symbol of completion, while 0 holds the dual meaning of new beginnings and emptiness.\n\nIn the smartphone era, Korean dream interpreters have begun incorporating contact lists, messenger IDs, and verification codes into their readings. The phone number remains the most resonant symbol — in modern life, it is the literal key to a relationship. Having it in a dream means the connection is open; losing it signals the connection at risk.",
