    "culturalContext": "In Korean, the character 만 (萬) carries a cultural weight that reaches far beyond the literal number 10,000. More than the quantity itself, it is the concept it represents — 'all things,' 'infinite multitude,' and 'the highest possible fullness' — that is deeply woven into Korean language, ceremony, and daily life.\n\nThe most resonant expression is 만세 (萬歲), literally 'live for ten thousand years,' which was the supreme acclamation for emperors and kings throughout East Asia. In Korea, after the March 1st Independence Movement of 1919, 만세 was also seared into the national soul as a cry of dignity and liberation. Today it lives on as a universal exclamation of joy and victory — shouted when a team wins a championship, when an exam is passed, when wedding toasts are raised.\n\n만물 (萬物) means 'all things in the universe.' As expressed in the Tao Te Ching, the entirety of creation flows through 만물 — the completed world as the ultimate product of existence. In Korean traditional thought, understanding the principle of 만물 was the ultimate aspiration of the cultivated person of virtue (군자).\n\n만복 (萬福) — 'ten thousand blessings' — was embroidered alongside longevity, good fortune, health, and peace motifs onto traditional Korean wedding quilts and folding screens. 만수무강 (萬壽無疆) — 'may your life be ten thousand years without boundary' — was the most formal and tender expression of longevity offered to elders at Korea's great milestone celebrations: 환갑 (60th birthday), 칠순 (70th), and 팔순 (80th).\n\nIn Korean folk dream interpretation, 만 is the supreme number of abundance — its appearance in a dream confirming that whatever is wished for will be realized ten-thousandfold. The everyday presence of the 만원 (10,000-won) banknote as Korea's most frequently used currency is itself a reflection of 만's deep symbolic meaning of 'sufficient fullness' carried into modern life.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychology offers several compelling frameworks for understanding why the number 10,000 — or its Korean equivalent 만 — appears in dreams with such force.\n\nFrom a Freudian perspective, 만 in dreams represents the extreme amplification of wish-fulfillment desire. When libidinal energy goes unsatisfied in waking life, the unconscious compensates by projecting the deficit onto an exaggerated quantity: the near-infinite magnitude of ten thousand. A dream of 만원 bills pouring out expresses the primal drive for material security; shouting 만세 may signal the dramatic discharge of suppressed desires for recognition or personal power. Freud would identify 만's very excessiveness as characteristic of the pleasure principle overriding the reality principle — the psyche granting itself what waking life withholds.\n\nJungian analytical psychology connects 만 (10,000) to the archetype of totality. Just as 만 refers to 만물 — the entirety of the universe — in East Asian philosophy, from a Jungian standpoint 만 symbolizes the Self: the total psyche encompassing all contents of both consciousness and the unconscious. The number's appearance in a dream may signal an important stage in the individuation process, where the Shadow, Anima or Animus, and the Self archetype are integrating into a unified whole. A dream of 만물 spreading before the dreamer carries particular resonance as the experience of an expanding circle of consciousness — a mandala-like totality of wholeness.\n\nModern cognitive psychology and neuroscience explain 만's dream appearances through the brain's activation of an abundance schema. Because people routinely use 10,000 as a goal unit — '10,000 won is enough,' 'I'll walk 10,000 steps,' 'I want to reach 10,000 followers' — this number is deeply embedded in memory structures and frequently surfaces during REM sleep's consolidation cycle. Emotionally charged expressions like 만세 are encoded deeply in the amygdala; during positive emotional states, the imagery of joy and liberation associated with 만 is highly likely to replay in dream form.\n\nIn the cross-cultural dimension, 만 is shared across East Asia as a symbol of infinite multitude: China's Great Wall (万里長城, literally 'ten-thousand-li wall') and Japan's Man'yōshū anthology (万葉集, 'countless leaves of poetry') carry the same resonance. In the modern era, the 10,000-steps walking goal — drawn directly from the Korean and Japanese wellness tradition — has become a globally recognized health benchmark, making 만 a number that now transcends any single culture.",
